Title:
Playing a Different Game: Rethinking Modern Defense Evasion

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post argues that modern EDRs have become highly effective at detecting “advanced” in-memory tradecraft (shellcode loaders, injection, sleep masking, stack spoofing), making the ROI of shellcode-centric implants increasingly poor.  
- The author explores an alternative execution model: persisting and executing a C2 agent entirely in JavaScript by backdooring trusted Electron applications (modifying JavaScript inside `app.asar` / `app.asar.unpacked` and commonly loaded `node_modules`).  
- A Mythic-compatible Node.js agent is built with HTTP/WebSocket comms, basic post-ex commands, file transfer, and SOCKS pivoting, then obfuscated to generate many static variants.  
- To regain low-level capability, the post introduces Node native addons (`.node` via N-API/node-gyp) and ports a COFF/BOF loader to execute Beacon Object Files from within the Electron/Node process.  
- The author compares detections: a traditional reflective/PIC beacon with evasion triggers multiple Elastic Defend alerts, while the “no injection, just JS inside Slack” approach yields a callback with no alerts in their test.  
- It’s useful for red teamers/payload developers evaluating non-shellcode tradecraft, and for defenders looking at Electron/Node supply-chain and runtime integrity gaps.

Technical Focus:
- Electron application backdooring via `app.asar` / `app.asar.unpacked` and `node_modules` tampering
- Node.js-based C2 agent design (HTTP, WebSocket, tasking, SOCKS proxy)
- JavaScript obfuscation for static variance (javascript-obfuscator)
- Node native addons (`.node`) using N-API and `require()` loading behavior
- COFF/BOF in-process execution via a native COFF loader addon
- Detection tradeoffs vs classic in-memory evasion (injection, reflective loading, sleep masking, stack spoofing)

Use Cases:
- Backdoor persistence/execution by modifying Electron app resources without touching signed PEs
- Running a Mythic agent in a “managed runtime” (Node.js) to avoid shellcode/injection telemetry
- WebSocket-based SOCKS pivoting with reduced noisy HTTP polling
- Extending JS implants with native `.node` modules for Windows API access and BOF execution
- Defensive validation: monitoring Electron resource integrity and native module loads from user-writable paths

Keywords:
Electron, Node.js, JavaScript, JScript, app.asar, app.asar.unpacked, node_modules, supply-chain tampering, Mythic C2, WebSocket C2, HTTP beaconing, SOCKS5 proxy, javascript-obfuscator, N-API, node-gyp, native addon, .node module, require(), COFF loader, BOF, Beacon Object Files, TrustedSec COFFLoader, Sliver armory, Elastic Defend, EDR evasion, reflective loading, sleep masking, stack spoofing, indirect syscalls